Z||000019||SteveJ||08-29-2001||07:59 AM||||Well over 50% of first generation Neons blow the head gasket. Some have said as high as 75%. Chrysler brought out a "multi layer steel" gasket in Nov of 98 as a cure so the problem is fixed from the middle of the 99 model run. If the car has less than 100k and wasn't abused Chrysler will do it for a copay of about $100. <BR>When considering a used Neon the first thing to look for is the tabs of the gasket sticking out with a rivit holding the layers of the gasket together.<P>Just as with a 4 cylinder Fiero you gotta have a manual to get performance. . .and don't forget these babys can get 40 MPG on the road with the manual. It's a high reving engine so automatic users don't see it's best qualities. ( Unlike GM's 2.2 which produces a flat torque curve at low rpm )<P>A 1995 model car for $2000. Yea, I like that.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Formula||08-29-2001||08:56 AM||||actually its a 96 wih 60,000 miles.<P>the only thing that has ever been wrong with it was the ac compressor.  He had the recall done to the headgasket already so Im not worried about that.<P>I'm puting all highway miles on it to drive to school.  The wheels that are on it were $1000 and he has over $1000 in body work done to it and has never had any major problems.  it was a good deal to me!  <P>When I compared the fiero and the neon saying they are pretty close I meant..<P>neon=30+ mpg<BR>fiero=9 mpg (mine 3.2 stroker carbed)<P>neon= holds books in back seat, so you can have a passenger<BR>fiero= books or passenger, trunk is filled ith tools<P>neon= this one turns a lot of heads<BR>fiero= this one turns a lot of heads<P>neon= 130 hp (lack of power for city driving (I drive on the highway al the time to school)<BR>fiero= 270 dynoed hp gobs of power for anything... the fiero would EAT the neon<P>neon= not the most fun car to drive but its not bad because it turns heads, I know its going to get me there and back though<BR>fiero= extremly fun, but always afraid that its not going to get me home<P>This is how I se it..... I still have a top of the line Fiero but I have a beater car too.  anyone who says they hate a car without driving, or seeing it in this case is doing the same thing that most retards do to the fiero. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||firstfiero||08-29-2001||10:22 AM||||      I'm not talking about money....The head gasket SHOULDN'T HAPPEN! It's not that a gasket is an expensive thing for any car...it's that you shouldn't need a new one already.<P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||SteveJ||08-29-2001||12:41 PM||||The failure is oil dribbling out the head gasket down the side of the block. They have an oil supply going up to the overhead cam that runs right next to the edge of the block and is very close to the edge. Apparently not enough gasket to hold the pressure. So it was a design problem in the block and the head. The new gasket works fine though.||||reg||1
